// Case study schedule #2: distributed — 4 compute workers, row-band
// partition, async input streaming, sync output write-back.
//
// This is the PRODUCTION DECOMPOSITION the case study witnesses. The
// outer y-loop is partitioned across 4 compute workers as contiguous
// row bands (partition=rows); the host owns I/O and broadcasts/gathers.
//
// WHY THE GATE CARRIES THIS AT VGA SIZE (the keystone point)
// ----------------------------------------------------------
// A single-pass stencil's cross-worker transfers (img_in host->workers,
// img_out workers->host) are LOOP-DEPTH-0: one Push + one Wait per
// shared buffer place, fired once, NOT inside an iteration Repeat.
// That is the single-shot matched-pair subclass the symbolic soundness
// gate (TASK-0455.01) proves bounded + deadlock-free FLAT IN N (peak
// occupancy = 1 <= cap, on the ROLLED ACFG, without expanding the net).
// So this schedule gates at VGA size in a few MB of RSS, where the
// expanded-replay gate projects roughly 4-12GB at THIS workload's
// size (19.6M nodes; the ~125GB figure belongs to the N=512 matmul,
// a different net — wave-8 review consistency fix).
//
// CAPABILITY SURFACE (which backends carry this cell)
// ---------------------------------------------------
// `transfer img_in : async, buffer=2, notify=event` requires async +
// buffer + event notification. Backends that DO satisfy it:
//   - pthreads-async  (tier-1, shared-memory threads)        [runner arm 1]
//   - mpi-nonblocking (tier-2, MPI_Ibsend/Imrecv, .#mpi)     [runner arm 2]
//   - mp-tcp-event / mp-uds-event (tier-1, event reactors)
// Backends that REJECT it at the capability check (sync / single-buffer
// / no-event): pthreads-sync, mp-tcp-bufsync, mp-tcp-poll, openmp-rs,
// mpi-blocking. The rejection is loud and pre-codegen, by design (the
// schedule names a capability the backend's capabilities.toml does not
// list). The runner picks pthreads-async + mpi-nonblocking — one
// tier-1 and one tier-2 arm — and asserts both are byte-identical to
// the independent reference oracle.
//
// ROW-BAND DIVISIBILITY (load-bearing math)
// -----------------------------------------
// The compute loop walks y in 1..H-1 = 1..15359 (length 15358). The
// partition_rows pass splits 15358 rows across 4 workers with the
// numpy.array_split floor-with-spillover remainder policy (TASK-0262):
// 15358 = 4*3839 + 2, so the first 2 workers get 3840 rows and the last
// 2 get 3839 (bands of length 3840/3840/3839/3839). halo_y = 1
// (inferred from blur3's 3x3 access on img_in) extends each band by one
// row on each interior side, so each worker's img_in tile is its band
// + halo.
//
// WIRE NARROWING (the transfer-volume story; TASK-0453.22, landed)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------
// Both edges are contiguous row spans (RecvBasis::Flat), so the wire
// flip narrows them from the whole ~39MB array to each worker's band:
// img_in host->workers transmits only the band+halo, img_out
// workers->host transmits only the interior band. The runner records
// the measured per-edge byte volume. Per-worker MEMORY is still
// full-shaped (the receiver allocates the whole [N] dest and pastes the
// band in) — the honest footprint residual, filed as TASK-0455.14.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    place load_image on host;
    place save_image on host;
    place blur3      on { w0, w1, w2, w3 };

    // Outer y-loop: row-band partition across the 4 compute workers.
    // The halo (one row each side) is inferred from blur3's access
    // pattern, NOT declared in the schedule (PRD §6.3.3).
    loop y : partition=rows;

    // host -> workers: pipelined async streaming, two row-bands in
    // flight. Event-driven notification — a sync-only backend rejects
    // this schedule at compile time, by design.
    transfer img_in  : async, buffer=2, notify=event;

    // workers -> host: blocking write-back. Host waits for all workers
    // to finish before save_image runs.
    transfer img_out : sync;
}
